"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[9545],{9545:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-06","slug":"base-06","trackId":"base","order":7,"title":"DeFi, Smart Contracts and Staking","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":13,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-07"],"markdown":"Lesson 6 \xb7 9 min\\n\\n## DeFi and Staking\\n\\nSmart Contracts, DeFi and Staking in Plain Language\\n\\nSo far, we have mainly discussed storing and transferring cryptocurrency. A wallet can do more than move money between addresses. It can enable swaps, borrowing, interest on assets and other financial tools, without requiring a bank.\\n\\nHow is this possible? Through smart contracts.\\n\\n### 1. What Is a Smart Contract?\\n\\nDespite the name, it contains no intelligence.\\n\\nA smart contract is a program that automatically executes predefined rules.\\n\\nThink of a vending machine. You insert money, select a drink and press a button. The machine checks the amount and dispenses the drink if it is sufficient. No shop assistant or manual decision is needed.\\n\\nAll the rules are built into the machine. A smart contract works similarly. It may specify:\\n- If a user sends one asset, provide another.\\n- If a user supplies enough collateral, allow a certain amount to be borrowed.\\n- If specified conditions are met, credit a reward.\\n\\nOnce the conditions are met, the program performs the prescribed actions automatically.\\n\\nThere is an important distinction, however.\\n\\nThe blockchain network verifies execution. Operations can therefore follow predetermined rules without trusting an individual employee or company. A smart contract is not necessarily secure, though. Code can contain bugs, and some contracts let developers change rules or control users’ funds.\\n\\nAutomatic execution is not a reason to trust a program unconditionally.\\n\\nTap to zoom in\\n\\n### 2. Why Smart Contracts Are Useful\\n\\nThe traditional financial system usually involves an intermediary. A bank or exchange office converts currency. A bank issues a loan. A bank also accepts interest-bearing deposits.\\n\\nThe financial institution holds the money, sets rules and determines which operations are permitted. Smart contracts transfer some functions to software. Code, rather than a bank employee, checks transaction conditions. It can check whether collateral is sufficient for a loan, calculate interest and determine repayment requirements.\\n\\nMuch of what is called DeFi is built on this technology.\\n\\n### 3. What Is DeFi?\\n\\nDeFi, Decentralized Finance, consists of financial services based on blockchains and smart contracts.\\n\\nImagine an ordinary banking app. It lets you:\\n- exchange currencies;\\n- deposit money for interest;\\n- borrow;\\n- transfer funds;\\n- use other financial products.\\n\\nNow imagine similar capabilities, with smart contracts performing much of the work instead of a bank. This is DeFi’s central idea. Users can:\\n- swap one cryptocurrency for another;\\n- supply assets for lending;\\n- borrow against cryptocurrency collateral;\\n- earn income from supplying funds;\\n- participate in other financial mechanisms.\\n\\nOpening a bank account or registering with every service is not necessarily required. In many cases, connecting a cryptocurrency wallet is sufficient. But there is a fundamental difference: when something goes wrong in a conventional bank, a company, employees and support staff are usually available.\\n\\nIn DeFi, user error or a smart-contract vulnerability can cause irreversible losses. Not every DeFi service is fully independent of its developers; some retain centralized controls. Choose platforms with great care.\\n\\n### 4. A Simple Example: DeFi Lending\\n\\nSuppose you have 1 000 USDC that you are not using. Instead of leaving it in the wallet, you can deposit it into a DeFi service. Other users can borrow from the service by supplying their own cryptocurrency as collateral. They pay interest for using borrowed funds.\\n\\nPart of that interest goes to the supplier. If a 5% annual yield remains unchanged, 1 000 USDC could earn about 50 USDC over a year before fees and potential losses. Conceptually, it resembles a bank deposit, but its inner workings differ.\\n\\nNo manager approves each loan manually. Smart contracts determine:\\n- the collateral required;\\n- how much can be borrowed;\\n- the interest payable;\\n- what happens if collateral value falls sharply.\\n\\nIf collateral becomes insufficient, the system may automatically sell part of it to repay debt. This enables lending without a traditional bank.\\n\\nBut it is not a bank deposit.\\n\\nYield can change, and government deposit insurance usually does not protect the funds. Software defects, collateral problems or other system failures can cause financial losses. Stablecoins do not make these operations risk-free.\\n\\n### 5. What Is Staking?\\n\\nStaking differs from the financial services above. In some blockchains, it is part of the mechanism supporting the network’s operation and security. Imagine participants checking transactions and enforcing rules.\\n\\nTo participate, they supply coins as a kind of accountability bond. Correct behavior can earn rewards. Breaking certain rules can result in losing part of those coins. Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab) uses such a mechanism.\\n\\nSolana ↗ (opens in a new tab) also uses staking, although its operating and penalty rules differ.\\n\\nWhy is this necessary?\\n\\nA blockchain needs to decide which transactions are valid and defend against deception. Staking helps organize that work. Ordinary users need not maintain hardware and verify transactions themselves. They can delegate coins to technical operators and receive part of the rewards.\\n\\nSome networks lock coins for a specified period; others have more flexible arrangements.\\n\\n#### Staking Is Not a Bank Deposit\\n\\nAt first, it looks straightforward:\\n\\nDeposit coins → receive a few percent annually.\\n\\nAn important detail changes the picture. Suppose you hold $1 000 in SOL and stake it at 7% annually. After a year, your coin quantity increases by roughly 7%.\\n\\nBut SOL’s price falls by 50% over that year. You now have more coins, yet their total dollar value falls to about $535.\\n\\nYou gained coins but lost almost half your money in dollar terms.\\n\\nThe interest rate alone therefore tells you nothing about the actual return. Also, not every product called “staking” is genuine blockchain staking. The term sometimes describes entirely different yield mechanisms.\\n\\nYou stake coins worth $1 000. What happens after a year? Yield 7% annually. Coin price change −50%.\\n\\n$535\\n\\nCoin quantity rises by 7%. More coins, less money: a loss of $465. A staking percentage does not tell you your dollar return.\\n\\n### 6. Where Yield Comes From and Why It Is Risky\\n\\nA beginner opening a DeFi service may see:\\n\\n5% annually.\\n\\n12% annually.\\n\\n30% annually.\\n\\nSometimes even 100% annually. An obvious question follows:\\n\\nwhy does everyone not simply deposit money and live on interest? Because free money is rare. Every yield has an economic mechanism behind it. For example:\\n\\n1. Loan interest\\n\\nSome users borrow and pay interest. Others supply funds and receive a share of it.\\n\\n2. Transaction fees\\n\\nUsers pay for swaps and other actions. Some fees may be distributed to those supplying the funds needed by the service.\\n\\n3. Blockchain-operation rewards\\n\\nIn some networks, participants receive new coins and a share of fees for helping secure the network.\\n\\n4. Rewards in a project’s own tokens\\n\\nA service may distribute tokens to attract users. These rewards are not necessarily backed by real profits. It may issue many tokens that depreciate over time. A high yield therefore does not always mean a large profit.\\n\\nImagine an offer of 50% annual rewards in a new project’s token. It sounds attractive. But if the token price falls by 80%, the rewards will not save the investment. The high rate may also last only while the project is attracting users.\\n\\nThe central rule: if you do not understand the source of yield, you do not understand the risk you are taking.\\n\\n#### Why Earning in DeFi Is Professional Territory\\n\\nLearning to use a wallet, hold stablecoins and make ordinary transfers can be relatively quick. Earning consistently through DeFi is a different matter. You need to understand the service, its operators, where the interest money comes from, what could happen under adverse conditions and how you would respond.\\n\\nEven modest-looking yields can involve substantial risks. High yields especially often carry additional risks. Do not treat DeFi as an easy passive-income source. It is a full financial industry, with complex mechanisms, competition and the possibility of losing money.\\n\\n### 7. Why You Must Not Confirm Every Request\\n\\nEveryone needs to understand another DeFi feature. After connecting a wallet, a financial service may ask you to:\\n- Connect — connect the wallet.\\n- Sign — sign a message.\\n- Approve — authorize the use of particular tokens.\\n- Confirm — confirm an operation.\\n\\nThese actions may look similar but have very different consequences. For example, swapping USDC for ETH may require permission to use your USDC.\\n\\nThat permission is called Approve. It lets a particular smart contract transfer your tokens within a specified allowance. Sometimes it covers only the required amount; sometimes it covers an unlimited quantity.\\n\\nGranting permission to a fraudulent or vulnerable contract can put your money at risk. Follow these rules:\\n- Use thoroughly verified services.\\n- Check the website address before connecting a wallet.\\n- Do not confirm requests you do not understand.\\n- Review permission amounts and conditions.\\n- Set the required amount yourself rather than granting unlimited access.\\n- Periodically review and revoke unnecessary permissions.\\n\\nRemember that an ordinary message signature is not always harmless. Some signatures can also authorize control of assets.\\n\\nDo not press Confirm merely because a website asks you to.\\n\\nIn cryptocurrency, security depends heavily on the user’s own actions.\\n\\n### 8. How Can You Learn More?\\n\\nDeFi is an entire financial industry with major participants, business models, income sources and risks. The paid course examines the economics of the crypto market: who creates financial services, who uses them, where money comes from and how the largest projects earn revenue.\\n\\nIt also examines Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab), Solana ↗ (opens in a new tab), Uniswap ↗ (opens in a new tab), Chainlink ↗ (opens in a new tab), Jupiter ↗ (opens in a new tab) and other key projects: their design, economics, competitors and industry roles.\\n\\n### 9. The Essentials\\n\\nA smart contract automatically executes predefined rules. DeFi provides blockchain-based services for asset swaps, supplying and taking loans, and other financial tools. Staking participates in the operation of certain blockchains in return for rewards.\\n\\nCryptocurrency supports more than storing and transferring money: it offers a whole system of financial instruments. The more complex the instrument, the more important it is to understand how it works, where its yield comes from and which risks you accept.\\n\\nUnderstand the instrument before entrusting it with your money.\\n\\nThe next lesson examines crypto loans in more detail: why users borrow against their assets, where interest comes from and which risks arise.\\n\\n### Check Yourself 1 / 3\\n\\nWhat is a smart contract?\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Blockchain** — A record of all transactions that everyone can inspect: entries can be added, but old entries cannot be changed. It is stored simultaneously on millions of computers, making falsification of the history physically impossible\\n- **Smart Contract** — Code that runs automatically when a condition is met, without intermediaries or the option to change your mind. Like a vending machine, but for money and transactions\\n- **Stablecoin** — A cryptocurrency pegged one-to-one to the dollar (USDT, USDC). A “dollar flash drive”: transfer it to another country in a minute. More money passes through stablecoins than through Bitcoin\\n- **Staking** — “Locking” coins in a network as collateral in return for rewards (3–10% a year). Similar to a deposit, but you cannot sell the coins while they are staked, and their price can fall\\n- **Deposit Insurance** — A government guarantee to compensate a depositor up to a specified amount if a bank fails. In Russia, up to 1,4 million ₽ per bank through the deposit insurance agency\\n- **DeFi (Decentralized Finance)** — An attempt to rebuild the banking system with smart contracts: no banks, offices or employees, only code. It operates 24/7, but without insurance. Both a revolution and a casino\\n- **Ethereum** — Not a currency but a platform, a “world computer,” running smart contracts, DeFi and most stablecoins. Second-largest in size and trust\\n- **USDC** — Circle’s stablecoin, considered the most transparent: regular audits and clearly described reserves\\n- **Yield** — The annual percentage an investment actually earns at today’s price. A bond’s price and yield move in opposite directions: when the bond becomes cheaper, its yield rises. Government bond yields influence loan and deposit rates\\n- **Token** — A digital asset that exists within a network, such as USDT or USDC. The same token can exist on different networks, and those are different versions of it\\n\\n### End-of-Lesson Quiz (“Check Yourself”):\\n**Question 1: What is a smart contract?**\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 1: A program that automatically executes predefined rules\\n- ⚪ Option 2: A contract personally signed by a bank employee\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Artificial intelligence that decides whom to give money to\\n\\n**Question 2: You see a 30% annual DeFi yield. What should you ask first?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Whether leverage can earn even more\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: Where the yield comes from and which risks support it\\n- ⚪ Option 3: How quickly you can transfer all your capital there\\n\\n**Question 3: Staking increased your SOL quantity by 7%, but SOL’s price fell by 50%. What happened?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: You still earned 7% in dollars\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: Your coin quantity increased, but your dollar capital value may have fallen substantially\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Staking guarantees recovery of the original value\\n\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-06-1","prompt":"What is a smart contract?","options":[{"id":"base-06-1-1","label":"A program that automatically executes predefined rules","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. A program that automatically executes predefined rules"},{"id":"base-06-1-2","label":"A contract personally signed by a bank employee","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. A program that automatically executes predefined rules"},{"id":"base-06-1-3","label":"Artificial intelligence that decides whom to give money to","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. A program that automatically executes predefined rules"}],"explanation":"A program that automatically executes predefined rules","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1283–1286 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-06-2","prompt":"You see a 30% annual DeFi yield. What should you ask first?","options":[{"id":"base-06-2-1","label":"Whether leverage can earn even more","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Where the yield comes from and which risks support it"},{"id":"base-06-2-2","label":"Where the yield comes from and which risks support it","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Where the yield comes from and which risks support it"},{"id":"base-06-2-3","label":"How quickly you can transfer all your capital there","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Where the yield comes from and which risks support it"}],"explanation":"Where the yield comes from and which risks support it","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1288–1291 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-06-3","prompt":"Staking increased your SOL quantity by 7%, but SOL’s price fell by 50%. What happened?","options":[{"id":"base-06-3-1","label":"You still earned 7% in dollars","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Your coin quantity increased, but your dollar capital value may have fallen substantially"},{"id":"base-06-3-2","label":"Your coin quantity increased, but your dollar capital value may have fallen substantially","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Your coin quantity increased, but your dollar capital value may have fallen substantially"},{"id":"base-06-3-3","label":"Staking guarantees recovery of the original value","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Your coin quantity increased, but your dollar capital value may have fallen substantially"}],"explanation":"Your coin quantity increased, but your dollar capital value may have fallen substantially","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1293–1296 (Verbatim)"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":1062,"lineEnd":1301}}')}}]);